An escape survives in the SECOND paragraph of a task item, and in a nested
task item, not only in the first line of a top level one.

These are the two places where escaping and task list support meet. A
continuation paragraph is a separate child of the item, so it takes its own
trip through the CommonMark writer; a nested item is reached by a recursive
walk. Either path could hand on the text literal unescaped while the simple
case kept working.

The item text also carries a checkbox that is not a checkbox: `\[x\]` in
the prose of the last item is escaped AND late, so it must neither be read
as the box nor tick the real one in front of it.
